<article class="env-remark" id="2AEFC9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/2AEFC9">Remark <span data-tag="2AEFC9">1.2.2.4</span>.</a> If $S$ is a simplicial set and $x,y,z \in S_0$, then there is no obvious composition law </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[ \Hom ^{\rght }_{S}(x,y) \times \Hom ^{\rght }_{S}(y,z) \rightarrow \Hom ^{\rght }_{S}(x,z). \]
</div>
  <p> We will later see that if $S$ is an $\infty $-category, then there is a composition law which is well-defined up to a contractible space of choices. The absence of a canonical choice for a composition law is the main drawback of $\Hom ^{\rght }_{S}(x,y)$, in comparison with $\bHom _{\sCoNerve [S]}(x,y).$ The main goal of §<a href="/tag/B22CC5" data-tag="B22CC5">2.2</a> is to show that, if $S$ is an $\infty $-category, then there is a (canonical) isomorphism between $\Hom ^{\rght }_{S}(x,y)$ and $\bHom _{\sCoNerve [S]}(x,y)$ in the homotopy category $\calH $. In particular, we will conclude that $\Hom ^{\rght }_{S}(x,y)$ represents $\bHom _{S}(x,y)$, whenever $S$ is an $\infty $-category. </p>
</article>